<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yongle Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1360">1360</a>–<a href="/years/1424">1424</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yongle Emperor was the third emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a>, reigning from 1402 to 1424 after seizing the throne from his nephew. He moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, began construction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a> and campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>. Under his reign maritime commerce reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> through the voyages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor.jpg" alt="Yongle Emperor" width="289" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_assis_de_l%27empereur_Ming_Chengzu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1414 envoys from Bengal presented a giraffe from East Africa to the <b>Yongle Emperor</b> in China, where it was hailed as a qilin, a mythical creature of good omen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1407 the armies of the <b>Ming Yongle Emperor</b> overthrew the short-lived Hồ dynasty and annexed Đại Việt as the province of Jiaozhi, imposing heavy taxes and labour levies and carrying books and craftsmen off to China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-le-dynasty">Later Le dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The chief enemy was Ayutthaya, whose ambitions drove the king to China in 1418 to seek protection, and the <b>Yongle Emperor</b> warned the Siamese ruler in October 1419.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Domestic markets thrived, and in the early 15th century the <b>Yongle Emperor</b> sponsored Zheng He's voyages to Southeast Asia, India and East Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Chinese records show that in 1417 three Sulu rulers, among them Paduka Batara, led a large mission to the Ming court of the <b>Yongle Emperor</b>; Paduka Batara died on the return journey and was buried at Dezhou in Shandong, where his tomb still stands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sulu-sultanate">Sulu Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It could also be won by war, as the <b>Yongle Emperor</b> took it from his nephew in 1402, and lost by capture: when the Oirats seized the Zhengtong Emperor in 1449 his brother took the throne, and he won it back only by a coup in 1457.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ming-china">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</a></li></ul></section>
